  "name": "B CELL REARRANGEMENT S IGH CLONALITY BY PCR - BONE MARROW",
  "code": "BCR_IGH_PCR",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<ul>\n<li data-start=\"201\" data-end=\"322\">\n<p data-start=\"203\" data-end=\"322\">This test detects <strong data-start=\"221\" data-end=\"277\">immunoglobulin heavy chain (IGH) gene rearrangements</strong> using <strong data-start=\"284\" data-end=\"319\">Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"323\" data-end=\"424\">\n<p data-start=\"325\" data-end=\"424\">Normal B-cells undergo gene rearrangements during maturation, leading to <strong data-start=\"398\" data-end=\"421\">polyclonal patterns</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"425\" data-end=\"568\">\n<p data-start=\"427\" data-end=\"568\">In <strong data-start=\"430\" data-end=\"479\">B-cell neoplasms (e.g., lymphomas, leukemias)</strong>, a single abnormal clone proliferates, resulting in a <strong data-start=\"534\" data-end=\"565\">clonal (monoclonal) pattern</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"569\" data-end=\"683\">\n<p data-start=\"571\" data-end=\"683\">Used to differentiate between <strong data-start=\"601\" data-end=\"645\">reactive (benign) lymphoid proliferation</strong> and <strong data-start=\"650\" data-end=\"680\">malignant B-cell disorders</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Result</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Meaning</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Polyclonal IGH rearrangement</strong></td>\n<td>Indicates a mixture of different B-cell populations. Suggests <strong>reactive/benign lymphoid proliferation</strong> (e.g., infection, autoimmune disease).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monoclonal IGH rearrangement</strong></td>\n<td>Indicates a single B-cell clone. Strongly suggests <strong>B-cell malignancy</strong> such as Non-Hodgkin&rsquo;s lymphoma, Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL), Multiple Myeloma, or other B-cell neoplasms.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Oligoclonal pattern</strong></td>\n<td>Sometimes seen in chronic immune stimulation or evolving clonal process. Requires correlation with clinical and pathological findings.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Negative / No detectable rearrangement</strong></td>\n<td>May occur if clonal population is too small to detect or if there are technical issues. Does not rule out malignancy&mdash;clinical and histopathological correlation essential.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"1573\" data-end=\"1604\"><strong data-start=\"1577\" data-end=\"1602\">Clinical Significance</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"1605\" data-end=\"1908\">\n<li data-start=\"1605\" data-end=\"1701\">\n<p data-start=\"1607\" data-end=\"1701\"><strong data-start=\"1607\" data-end=\"1629\">Helps in diagnosis</strong>: Distinguishes reactive lymphoid hyperplasia from malignant lymphoma.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1702\" data-end=\"1804\">\n<p data-start=\"1704\" data-end=\"1804\"><strong data-start=\"1704\" data-end=\"1723\">Prognostic role</strong>: Confirms clonal nature of disease in suspected lymphoproliferative disorders.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1805\" data-end=\"1908\">\n<p data-start=\"1807\" data-end=\"1908\"><strong data-start=\"1807\" data-end=\"1841\">Minimal Residual Disease (MRD)</strong>: Can be used to track clonal populations during/after treatment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
